    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Here Are Some of the Serious and Obscure Brands That EICMA 2026 Had To Offer – Worth Knowing About\nIneos Grenadier and Quartermaster to Be Made in the USA Starting in 2028\n6 photos\nPhoto: Ineos\nOn the market for about four years already, the Ineos Grenadier has managed to establish a sort of cult following for itself. Built as a modern-day off-road vehicle with heavy Land Rover influences, the nameplate managed to conquer first and foremost the hearts and minds of American buyers.\nIt is from there that the bulk of the sales, some 70 percent, come from. It kind of makes sense, then, why Ineos is about to move production to the USA.\nCurrently assembled in France, at the Hambach plant that a long time ago was responsible for putting together the smart city cars, the Grenadier SUV, but also its Quartermaster double-cab pickup version, will roll off American production lines as soon as 2028.\nThe British company, whose name is best known in the petrochemicals, specialty chemicals, and oil products industries, announced this week that it has already shortlisted several potential production sites in the U.S., and says it will announce the final location within the next few months.\nIneos never said directly how many Grenadiers and Quartermasters it sells on an yearly basis (the rumor mill puts that at around 10,000 units per year), but, as stated, the bulk of the orders, 70 percent of them, come from North America.\nIt makes sense, then, for the carmaker to pack up and leave for friendlier shores, looking not only to be closer to its main client pool, but also to tap into more markets as it seeks to expand its business. And so hyped are the Brits about it that they call the move the biggest moment in their history.\nAs for the Hambach facility it now operates, Ineos says it is discussing letting it go to Chinese auto giant BYD . Should the two parties reach an agreement on that, the SUV and truck will continue to be produced there in the medium term, probably until the U.S.-based facility is ready.\nAs a reminder of what the Grenadier is, we're dealing with a boxy SUV with an attitude that would never back out of a fight with the more established names of off-roading. Built on a steel ladder chassis employing long-travel suspension on both ends, it rocks a 3.0-liter straight-six engine sourced from BMW rated at 278 horsepower and 332 lb-ft of torque.\nOn the American market, Ineos lists four versions of the Grenadier at the time of writing. The main model, called, somewhat ironically, Station Wagon, goes for $71,750, while the Quartermaster pickup variant ups that to $86,300.\nAn intermediate version called Helmsman is listed at $78,995, and there is also a limited-edition version in the works. The Brits call it the Arcane Works Detour, and only 200 of them will be made.\nWhat is Arcane Works, you ask? That would be an Ineos offshoot that will handle special versions of the SUV, but will also work as a testbed for new technologies, systems, and designs for future vehicles.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nEditor's note: Gallery shows the 2027 Ineos Grenadier Helmsman special edition.\nineos ineos grenadier INEOS Quartermaster\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT Driven: 2026 Subaru Trailseeker (e-Outback) Proves the Boxer Engine Isn't Everything",
